<article class="env-proposition" id="C27FEE">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/C27FEE">Proposition <span data-tag="C27FEE">1.2.9.2</span> <span class="named">(<span class="cite">[<a href="/bibliography/joyalpub">joyalpub</a>]</span>)</span>.</a> Let $S$ and $K$ be simplicial sets, and $p: K \rightarrow S$ an arbitrary map. There exists a simplicial set $S_{/p}$ with the following universal property: </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[ \Hom _{\sSet }(Y, S_{/p}) = \Hom _{p}( Y \star K, S), \]
</div>
  <p> where the subscript on the right hand side indicates that we consider only those morphisms $f: Y \star K \rightarrow S$ such that $f|K = p$. </p>
</article>
<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      One defines $(S_{/p})_ n$ to be $\Hom _{p}(\Delta ^ n \star K, S)$. The universal property holds by definition when $Y$ is a simplex. It holds in general because both sides are compatible with the formation of colimits in $Y$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>